jacob rees-mogg is a boris johnson  loyalist he was backing the prime  minister right to the end writing in  today's sunday express he compares the  ousting of the prime minister to the  slaying of julius caesar and said that  those who betrayed boris will now seek  to neuter brexit well the brexit  opportunities minister now has to decide  who he wants to replace boris johnson he  joins us now  live thank you very much for being on  the programme  first things first it feels like pretty  much everyone in the conservative party  fancies a crack at the top job can you  rule yourself out  well um  john lydon better name as johnny rotten  has kindly  endorsed me but he's not a member of the  conservative party i was very flattered  that he should suggest my name no like  david davis i am looking for  a candidate who will deliver the  policies uh that will ensure a  successful conservative government that  is my interest in this leadership  campaign and have you made your mind up  then who you're backing  no not yet we're beginning to see people  come out and set out their stalls but we  need someone who will be clear about  their principles  who will not have any baggage says to  speak  and  who  will be able to win both with the  conservative party at large and in a  general election  which is obviously from a conservative  point of view not just in the party's  interest but in the national interest  okay and if you've got a short list  of people  um well we haven't yet got all the names  so i think we'll have to wait and see  what all the names are there are some  good candidates who have come forward um  i was sorry that ben wallace decided to  withdraw i thought he was potentially a  very good candidate  so we'll have to see where where we get  to uh in the coming hours and even  a couple of days  do you think that the next leader of the  conservative party needs to be someone  who backed brexit  i think it's very important that  brexit has been done legally but there  is a great deal of work to do to get the  benefits of brexit to make the uk a less  regulated freer market that isn't in  lockstep with the european union part of  that is delivering on the northern  ireland protocol and i think the bill  before parliament at the moment  is a very good way of doing that so it  needs to be somebody who will support  that  but in my current role  not all ministers have embraced with  enthusiasm the advantages of brexit  within their own departments and that's  really important because that's where  the advantages lie  who are you talking about  um well i'm talking about the retained  eu law bill where we're trying to ensure  that all uk law by um 2026 ideally  possibly 2028  should cease to have supremacy and  should be  in line with uk law and uk law should be  our ruling force and there have been  some departments um the treasury wants  to keep tax as something that is still  subject to the idea of supremacy of eu  law who haven't adopted that with  enthusiasm and therefore i am concerned  that there are obstacles  and it's important to have people who  are wholehearted uh in their support of  brexit  i mean russia soon to be fair did  actually vote for brexit he he believes  in brexit well i  i wasn't really referring to chancellor  himself i was referring to the  institution of the treasury which i  think is one of the problems that we  have  in this country i think as david davis  was saying the treasury has got our  economic policy wrong with its  high tax approach and its failure to  understand the benefits of growth  the treasury in the interim between  rishi sunak and nadim zahawi properly  taking control  managed an attempt to kibosh uh the  public sector fraud authority because  it's embarrassed about the fraud that  has taken place affecting the treasury  so i think the treasury as an  institution is a real problem  and i would hope that any future leader  will tackle that so that it's under  proper political control it's difficult  isn't it because you know there's been  an awful lot of scrutiny about tax  policy hasn't there um lots of  candidates throwing around some very  eye-catching uh tax cuts i'm sure go  down very well  with conservative party members and but  at the same time you know  tax costs cost money and whether it's  spending cuts or adding to the debt uh  looking at what just what richie sue  next said recently you know i have a  responsibility to make sure we don't  burden our children with a legacy of  debt that i didn't deal with and that's  why i've had to make some decisions  which i know are not always popular  are you concerned that actually you know  in the leadership contest in the quest  for popularity people are being a bit  irresponsible  well i think random tax cuts drawn out  of a hat is not the way to approach this  i think we need a well thought through  economic policy based on how we tackle  inflation  and i think there is a place for tax  cuts within that  that if you think what ronald reagan did  in the 1980s there was a tight monetary  policy there was tight control of  expenditure and there was lower taxes  but the tax lawyering was done on a  reform of taxation to make it simpler to  have fewer exemptions to taxation so  there were fewer  means of getting around it fewer means  of legitimately avoiding tax but with  lower rates  i i think that's what we should be  looking at rather than plucking random  tax cuts out of the air  but there are detailed things you can do  if you take fuel duty  and we pay more in tax on fuel because  of the way vat is geared as the price  goes up so the treasury makes a profit  from higher fuel prices  which actually is a burden on the  economy do we really want to do that is  the gain the treasury is making a  proportionate one does it really want to  get more tax on petrol as prices go up  now i just want to talk a little bit  about uh boris johnson and you've  obviously has been one of his staunchest  defenders you were there in downing  street listening to him as he gave his  resignation a statement  now last week you described boris  johnson's mistakes as prime minister as  relatively minor missteps it got taken  out of all proportion  i mean we're talking about the handling  of sexual assault here aren't we do you  think that sounds a bit flippant  no no i don't because i believe in the  principle that somebody is innocent  until proved guilty  and they the reports of what mr pincher  has done  sound very serious  they have gone from  sounding as if somebody was too drunk  and behaved badly to a sexual assault  that is a different  category and when the prime minister was  first talking about it or his spokesman  was first talking about it  it sounded like somebody who was very  drunk  but the principle of innocence until  proved guilty is is fundamental  uh and mr pinter is entitled to due  process that that's a constitutional  fundamental i don't think anyone is  necessarily saying i don't think  anyone's necessarily saying that chris  pincher wasn't entitled to due process  but i think it was rather whether or not  people were entitled to know the full  facts about what number 10 knew and when  when he was made  when he was given the job  well dare i say in this current  leadership election there is all sorts  of gossip running wild about all sorts  of people none of which is known are we  to be a society that whenever there is a  little bit of gossip  excludes people from their ability to be  a member of parliament i i think we need  facts and there weren't facts there was  a degree of gossip and there was the  foreign office incident which had been  closed down there had been an accepted  apology and and the difficulty with that  is that if an apology has been accepted  you don't know at that point how serious  it was it may turn out later that it was  more serious  but it was something that was was closed  the evidence wasn't there  for the prime minister in my mind to  have made a fair decision um otherwise  okay um interesting to hear your taker  on that definitely and just finally  there are reports that boris johnson is  thinking about creating some new periods  in the next few months so do you think  that's acceptable uh to do well he's a  caretaker prime minister  well  any privileges that are created in the  next few months if there are any and i  don't know about this it would have been  in the pipeline for a long time  appearages don't just get created  because the pm wakes up in the morning  and says i will have a peer the letters  patent do say their peerage is created  by the emotion of the queen  to get from  the initial idea through to her  majesty's emotion  is quite a long-winded process

